# Doctrine

Wyrd's doctrine is the set of rules that keep the system coherent as it grows. It
is the "why" behind the object model. The protocol spec
(`architecture/wyrd-design.md`) is the authority on the "what" and wins on any
conflict; this page is the public summary for developers and agents.

## One object model, no skipped layers

Wyrd is built around declared intent, organized into five layers. Each layer only
operates on the one below it.

<DataTable
  columns={[
    { header: 'Layer', role: 'name' },
    { header: 'Meaning' }
  ]}
  rows={[
    ['Core nouns', 'The durable concepts every other layer uses: Card, Spec, Run, Observation.'],
    ['Card kinds', 'Domain specializations of Card. The kind changes the spec, not the ontology.'],
    ['Foundations', 'The envelope, metadata, CardRef, relationship, status, and version.'],
    ['Services', 'Registry, storage, lineage, policy, install, audit, observability, evaluation, drift, runtime.'],
    ['Surfaces', 'HTTP, SDK, CLI, UI, MCP, IDE integrations, and generated docs.']
  ]}
/>

A service operates on foundations. A surface exposes a service. A card kind
specializes `Card`. Runtime details stay behind the contract that explains them.

## The four nouns come first

```text
Cards declare.       Specs define.        CardRefs connect.
Relationships explain.   Status tracks lifecycle.
Runs record execution.   Observations record measured facts.
Policy governs decisions.   Audit records accountability.
```

Everything new should first try to fit as a card kind, a spec field, a
relationship, a run, or an observation. You add a new noun only when those shapes
cannot express the concept without lying. This is why there is no `Tool`, `Skill`,
or `SubAgent` card kind: a tool is a runtime name, sub-agency is an Agent
referencing an Agent, and neither needs a new noun.

## Relationships are derived, never authored

Authors write `CardRef` values inside specs. The server derives relationships from
them, and lineage reads those relationships. Users never maintain a parallel
lineage graph. If you find yourself hand-editing edges, the model is being
bypassed.

## Surfaces project one contract

The SDK, HTTP API, CLI, UI, generated schemas, and MCP tools all project the same
card contract. A surface may add an ergonomic shortcut, but it does not invent new
shapes, rename durable fields, or expose server-internal state. What you declare in
Python is recognizable in the HTTP payload, the stored card, the generated schema,
and the MCP tool input.

## The implementation rule

Every implementation preserves these invariants:

- contracts are typed
- specs are declarative
- CardRefs are explicit
- relationships are derived
- artifacts are cards
- registration is centralized
- runtime behavior stays out of durable specs
- public surfaces use Wyrd vocabulary

A plan that violates one of these needs a named exception and a reason strong
enough to survive review.

